{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space E with its compatible Borel measurable structure and canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure λ = volume, every C² potential V : E → ℝ, and all real constants κ, η with κ ≥ 1 and η > 0, suppose that for every x, v ∈ E,\n    κ⁻¹ ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] ≤ ‖v‖².\nLet d = dim_ℝ E, s = √η, μ = λ.tilted(-V), and\n    C = ((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d,\n    A(y) = C ∫_E exp(-V(x) - ‖y-x‖²/(2η)) λ(dx),\n    Vη(y) = -log(A(y)),\n    R_y = μ.tilted(x ↦ -‖x-y‖²/(2η)).\nThe tilt means actual normalized exponential reweighting of the indicated base measure, or the zero measure on its nonintegrable branch. Vη is defined using the literal unnormalized volume convolution above: the input potential V retains its additive normalization; no assumption ∫ exp(-V) dλ = 1 is made. On the integrable branch, μ has density proportional to exp(-V) against λ, and R_y has density proportional to exp(-V(x)-‖x-y‖²/(2η)) against λ. The gradient ∇ of any scalar function is its real Hilbert Riesz representative of the Fréchet derivative; all vector integrals below are actual Bochner integrals.\nThe theorem asserts jointly that μ is a probability measure, Vη is C², and, for every y ∈ E, R_y is a probability measure, the identity function x ↦ x is in L²(R_y), ∇V is Bochner integrable with respect to R_y, and\n    ∇Vη(y) = ∫_E ∇V(x) R_y(dx).\nIn addition, there exists one measurable function p : E → E such that, for every y ∈ E,\n    p(y) + η ∇V(p(y)) = y.\nFor this same function p, define for all y, u ∈ E\n    ρ_y(u) = V(p(y)+s u) - V(p(y)) - s ⟨∇V(p(y)),u⟩,\n    r_y = (x ↦ s⁻¹(x-p(y)))_# R_y,\nwhere # denotes the actual measurable pushforward. Then, for every y ∈ E, all of the following hold jointly:\n    r_y = λ.tilted(u ↦ -(‖u‖²/2 + ρ_y(u))),\n    r_y is a probability measure,\n    ∇ρ_y ∈ L²(r_y),\n    ∇Vη(y) = ∇V(p(y)) + s⁻¹ ∫_E ∇ρ_y(u) r_y(du),\nand, for every z ∈ E,\n    ∇V(p(y)+s z) = ∇V(p(y)) + s⁻¹ ∇ρ_y(z).\nHere multiplication of a scalar and an E-valued vector is real scalar multiplication, and ∇ρ_y differentiates with respect to u with y fixed. The moment, selector, smoothness and mean identities are outputs, not supplied premises. η is any positive real number, without a cap or relation to κ. All displayed conclusions use this fixed input η and the same produced function p; the last identity quantifies over every z for each y.\nDimension zero is included: d = 0, C = 1, E is the singleton zero vector space, A(0) = exp(-V(0)), Vη(0) = V(0), p(0) = 0, ρ_0(0) = 0, all gradients and vector integrals are zero, and μ, R_0 and r_0 are the singleton probability measure. No strictly positive dimension is assumed.",
  "objects": "Inputs are E with its real Hilbert, complete, finite-dimensional and compatible Borel structures; a C² potential V : E → ℝ; real κ ≥ 1; and real η > 0. Defined objects are λ = volume, d = finrank_ℝ E, μ = λ.tilted(-V), the displayed scalar Gaussian prefactor C, the scalar volume convolution A : E → ℝ, Vη = -log A, and the measures R_y. The output selector p : E → E is measurable and solves the stated stationarity equation. For the same p, ρ : E → E → ℝ is defined, and r_y is the actual affine pushforward of R_y. Hilbert gradients are real Riesz representatives, and vector integrals are Bochner integrals.",
  "domains": "x, v, y, u and z range over all E. κ and η are real, d is the nonnegative finite real dimension, C and A(y) are real, and p(y), gradients and vector means lie in E. ρ_y differentiates in its second variable u. μ, R_y and r_y are measures on E. L² for id under R_y means the actual second moment ∫ ‖x‖² R_y(dx) is finite; L² for ∇ρ_y under r_y means actual square-integrability of the E-valued gradient. Every displayed vector expectation is a Bochner integral.",
  "quantifiers": "Universal inputs E, V, κ and η with the displayed structures and hypotheses. The Hessian bound is for every x and v. Let μ, C, A, Vη and R be fixed. The output conjunction contains probability of μ, C² smoothness of Vη, all-y probability/moment/first-integrability/mean assertions for R_y, and existence of one measurable p. This p satisfies its equation for all y before ρ and r are defined using it. The assertions about r and the decomposed mean are for all y, with the last pointwise gradient identity for all z within each y. There is no independently supplied p for each y.",
  "assumptions": "κ ≥ 1; V is C² on all E; κ⁻¹ ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] ≤ ‖v‖² for every x and v; η > 0; and the stated finite-dimensional real Hilbert/Borel structures. Consequently κ and √η are positive and their displayed inverses have their ordinary meanings. There is no upper bound on η or assumption connecting η to κ. No probability measure, selector, moment, mean identity, normalization of V, or differentiability of a convolution is supplied as a hypothesis.",
  "conclusion": "μ is a probability measure and Vη is C². For every y, R_y is a probability measure, id belongs to L²(R_y), ∇V is Bochner integrable under R_y, and ∇Vη(y) equals its R_y-mean. A single measurable p is produced with p(y)+η ∇V(p(y))=y for all y. For that p and the defined ρ/r, every r_y is exactly the volume tilt by -(‖u‖²/2+ρ_y(u)), is a probability measure, and has ∇ρ_y in L². The displayed decomposition of ∇Vη into ∇V(p(y)) plus η^(-1/2) times the r_y-mean of ∇ρ_y holds, as does the pointwise gradient relation at every scaled z. No uniqueness or differentiability of p is explicitly part of the output, and no quantitative moment bound is stated.",
  "scopes": "The input η fixes the denominator 2η in R, the Gaussian prefactor, the smoothing potential Vη and the scale s = √η throughout the entire conclusion. κ is fixed in the global lower Hessian bound and is not a parameter varying with x or y. The produced p is one function, shared by stationarity, centering of ρ, the affine pushforward, the mean decomposition and the final pointwise identity. In ∇ρ_y, y is fixed and differentiation is with respect to u. Equality of r_y is equality of actual measures, not only equality of a selected moment. Dimension zero is included and C uses exponent zero there.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The Hessian lower constant is exactly κ⁻¹ and the upper constant exactly 1. C depends on η and d through ((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d, with π the usual real constant. μ depends on E/volume and V; R_y additionally depends on η and y. A and Vη use the original unnormalized volume density exp(-V), including the additive normalization of V. In density notation, their volume normalizer is determined by V and λ, not supplied. The existential p may depend on the fixed E, V and η and their hypotheses; the theorem asserts no unique selection or quantitative dependence on κ. ρ_y and r_y use that p, η and y. No new adjustable constant or step-size cap is produced or assumed.",
